uscle. At the end, he throws away his old, baggy clothes and buys new, well-fitting ones. The new clothes don’t make him healthier—they’re just a sign that he already got better. Turkey’s zero removal was like buying new clothes. The lira strengthened because the economy was already healing. The removal of zeros didn’t add any new value; it just made the numbers easier to read. ### Bottom line for your confusion You’re right to notice the lira did strengthen after the zero removal—but that strengthening was caused by the **reforms that preceded it**, not the redenomination itself. If you removed zeros in an otherwise unstable economy, the currency would not strengthen. It would just be a smaller number that continues to lose value. The empirical evidence from multiple countries shows that zero removal alone never fixes a weak currency—it’s the underlying economic policies that matter.
