00:32:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right to trust your memory—news segments often *do* show a currency strengthening immediately after a redenomination, and it’s easy to conclude that the zeros removal caused it. Let me walk through why that perception is so convincing, yet still misleading. ### Why the lira *appeared* to strengthen right after the zero removal In January 2005, when Turkey launched the New Turkish Lira, the exchange rate was set at exactly 1.35 YTL per dollar (the old rate of 1,350,000 old lira per dollar, divided by 1,000,000). But in the days and weeks that followed, the YTL actually appreciated to around 1.18–1.20 per dollar. A news segment covering that would naturally say: “The new lira has strengthened since the zeros were removed.” That’s factually accurate, but it misses the real driver. ### What the news segment probably didn’t show At the same time Turkey removed zeros, the central bank **raised interest rates** (from about 14% to 16% in early 2005). This attracted foreign investors looking for high yields. Those investors sold dollars and bought lira, pushing the exchange rate up. The zero removal was one of several events happening simultaneously, but the interest rate hike was the *direct* cause of the lira’s rise. Why would a news segment focus on the zero removal instead of the interest rate hike? Because “Turkey cuts zeros from currency” is a dramatic, visual story—you can show old notes with many zeros and new notes with few. “Turkey raises interest rates by 2%” is dry and technical. Producers choose the more exciting narrative. ### A simple test: Would the same happen if they removed zeros *without* the rate hike? Imagine Turkey had removed zeros but kept interest rates low. We can test this with other countries: - **Zimbabwe (2006, 2008, 2009):** Removed zeros multiple times. The currency never strengthened—it collapsed. Why? No accompanying tightening of monetary policy. - **Argentina (1992):** Removed zeros (replacing the austral with the peso at 1:10,000). The peso strengthened temporarily, but only because it was pegged to the dollar and backed by strict convertibility laws—again, not the zeros themselves. So the common factor is **policy reform**, not the zero cut. ### Why your memory is still valuable You’re not wrong that the lira strengthened *after* the zeros were cut. But the causal chain is: 1. Government implements tough economic reforms (2001–2004) → inflation falls, confidence rises. 2. As a final step, they remove zeros (2005) → this is a **signal** that reform is complete. 3. Central bank then raises interest rates (or leaves them high) → foreign capital floods in → lira strengthens. The zero cut was like a graduation ceremony. The graduation *happens* right before the student gets a job, but it’s the years of study that earned the job, not the ceremony itself. News cameras show the ceremony and say “Graduate gets job offer!” – but that’s correlation, not causation. ### Bottom line If you search news archives from early 2005, you’ll find articles saying “New Turkish Lira rises on confidence after zero-lopping.” But if you look at the same newspapers’ business sections, they’ll also mention “Central bank holds rates high, attracting foreign investors.” The two stories are connected, but the zero removal didn't *cause* the rise—it just happened to coincide. The real cause was investor confidence in Turkey’s *entire* reform package, which the zero removal symbolized. Does that help reconcile what you saw with the underlying economics? I know it’s frustrating when a vivid news memory seems to contradict a more abstract explanation. But economics is full of such illusions—changing the unit of measurement often gets mistaken for changing the value itself.
