ion effect only works when the redenomination is **backed by reality**. If the economy is still a mess, the zero removal is like a bankrupt person printing new business cards—it doesn’t change his bank balance. ### Your intuition about “looking stable” is half-right In the cases where redenomination *did* boost confidence (Turkey, Brazil, Poland), it wasn’t the zero removal itself that created trust. It was the fact that the government had already: - Slashed inflation from triple digits to single digits - Balanced the budget - Made the central bank independent - Eliminated money printing as a tool The zero removal was the **final exclamation point**—a way to say, “We’re serious this time.” And because investors already believed in the reforms, they saw the new currency as a fresh start, and they bought it. But imagine if Turkey had removed zeros in 2001, when inflation was 70% and the government was still running deficits. Would the lira have strengthened? Almost certainly not. It would have been like the old Turkish lira with six fewer zeros—still weak. ### So can we find a middle ground? You are correct that **perceived stability can lead to real currency appreciation**. The mechanism works like this: - Redenomination → looks like a clean break from past instability → attracts foreign investment → increased demand for the currency → stronger exchange rate. But the chain only holds if the **first link** (the clean break) is credible. If the economy is still inflationary, investors will bet against the new currency, and it will fall. ### Think of it this way Removing zeros is like a company changing its name after a scandal. If the company has genuinely reformed its practices, the new name can help rebuild trust. But if the same bad management and corruption remain, investors will just say, “Same old company, new name.” The name change doesn’t fix the underlying problems. So your feeling that zero removal can make a currency “look” more stable has a real basis—but it’s the **look** that matters, not the **reality**. And that look only works if the reality is already improving. If you cut zeros while inflation is still raging, the "look" is just a mask that quickly slips off. Does that help reconcile your intuition with the economic logic? You're not wrong that perception matters—it’s just that perception alone can't override weak fundamentals for long.
